Experiment 2: Exploratory Data Analysis on a Real-World Dataset Program: 
# Import the Pandas library import pandas as pd 
# Load the Titanic dataset data = pd.read_csv("titanic.csv") 
# Display the first five rows of the dataset print("First Five Records") print(data.head()) 
# Display basic information about the dataset print("\nDataset Information") print(data.info()) 
# Display the total number of missing values in each column print("\nMissing Values") print(data.isnull().sum()) 
# Display summary statistics of numerical columns print("\nSummary Statistics") print(data.describe()) 
# Display the names of all columns print("\nColumn Names") print(data.columns)

Experiment 3: Working with NumPy Arrays and Pandas DataFrames Program: 
# Import NumPy Library import numpy as np 
# Import Pandas library import pandas as pd 
# Create a NumPy array numbers = np.array([10, 20, 30, 40, 50]) print("NumPy Array") print(numbers) 
# Add 5 to each element in the array print("\nArray after adding 5") print(numbers + 5) 
# Create a dictionary student = {    "Name": ["Arun", "Bala", "Charan"],    "Age": [20, 21, 22],    "Marks": [85, 90, 88] } 
# Convert dictionary into DataFrame df = pd.DataFrame(student) print("\nStudent DataFrame") print(df) 
# Display only the Name column print("\nStudent Names") print(df["Name"]) 
# Add a new column df["Grade"] = ["A", "A+", "A"] print("\nUpdated DataFrame") print(df)
